# What a fuzzing run produces, none of which is source.
#
# `corpus/` is the inputs libfuzzer has kept because they reached new coverage.
# It grows every run - 3.7 MB after one twenty-second campaign across sixteen
# targets - and it is worth keeping on the machine that fuzzes, because a corpus
# is where the next run starts from. It is not worth keeping in the repository,
# where it would be a megabyte of churn per run for inputs nobody reads.
#
# `artifacts/` is the input that crashed a target. That one *is* worth keeping,
# and the way to keep it is the rule in README.md: add it as a case in the
# stable counterpart, written out in the test, so it is checked by every run of
# the ordinary suite rather than only while somebody is fuzzing. A file sitting
# here is checked by nobody.
corpus/
artifacts/
coverage/
target/
